## Verification: HARD RULES, no exceptions

[Your name] [can / cannot] catch a false "done" by reading the code. Treat each of these as a
blocker, not a reminder.

1. **Never write "verified", "working", "fixed", "done" or "confirmed" without pasting the
   command output that proves it, in the same reply.** No output means say what you did and
   did not check. "The code looks right" is not verification.
2. **[Your typecheck / lint / compile step] is not [what it doesn't prove].** [e.g. "A green
   typecheck proves the types line up, not that the page looks right. UI work is done only
   after the page is rendered and looked at."]
3. **Committed is not shipped.** [Where the live thing is actually tested, e.g. "A change is
   done only after deploy AND confirming it's in the live output." If there is no deploy yet:
   "There is no deploy yet; until there is, done means rendered and reviewed, not committed."]
4. **Every change to [your source folder, e.g. `src/`] goes through `code-reviewer` before it's
   called done.** A review verdict is part of the evidence, not optional polish.
5. **Never run two [dev servers / builds / test runs / deploys] at once.** They clobber each
   other's output and the wrong one gets checked.
6. **[Any gap between your test environment and production, e.g. "A green suite cannot see
   the live DB; check a real copy before trusting anything recently added." Delete if none.]**
7. **If you did not check it, say so.** An honest gap is always cheaper than a wrong
   confirmation.

**Rule 1 is enforced by a Stop hook**, `.claude/hooks/verify-claims.py`. If a reply claims
something was tested / verified / deployed and no matching command ran anywhere in the
session, the turn is blocked and you're told which check is missing. Denials ("not yet
verified") and quoted words never trip it. Run the check or state the gap. Do not reword the
claim to slip past it.

## Models

Deeper model where a mistake is expensive, faster model where it is cheap to undo.

| Work | Model |
|------|-------|
| Building, editing, running, small fixes | Sonnet |
| Planning, phases, architecture, reviewing a finished phase, hard debugging | Opus |
| A problem both have already failed on more than once | Fable |

Subagents pin their own model in their frontmatter (`code-reviewer`, `security-reviewer` and
`ux-reviewer` on Opus; `builder`, `debugger` and `responsive-reviewer` on Sonnet). Say which model ran the session in the
`/wrap-up` recap. If a planning or review task arrives on the fast model, say so rather than
proceeding quietly.

