## Claude Code tooling

Project-level config lives in `.claude/`. Machine-specific overrides go in
`.claude/settings.local.json` (gitignored via `*.local`). Never commit keys there.

**Workflow commands** (`.claude/commands/`)

| Command | Use it for |
|---|---|
| `/lead <task>` | Lead-developer mode: triage, delegate to subagents, review, report |
| `/clarify <task>` | Ask questions + produce a plan before any code is written |
| `/spec <feature>` | Write an implementation spec to `.ai/specs/<slug>.md` |
| `/task <spec> TN` | Implement exactly one task from a spec |
| `/review [target]` | Senior-engineer review of changed files |
| `/commit` | Draft a conventional commit message for staged changes |
| `/wrap-up` | End-of-session: update `.ai/STATUS.md`, verify, commit, push, name the model |
| `/explore <task>` | Parallel codebase exploration before a larger change |
| `/issue-read <#>` · `/fix-issue <#>` · `/pr-summary` | GitHub issue/PR helpers (need `gh` CLI) |

Typical feature flow: `/lead <task>` for anything touching `[your source folder]`; or manually
`/clarify` → `/spec` → `/task … T1` → `/review` → `/commit` per task.

**Delegation policy: act as lead developer**

Every task that touches `[your source folder]` is routed through the subagents below. The main
session plans, delegates, verifies, and reports rather than writing application code directly.
Use `/lead <task>` for the full orchestration flow, but apply the same routing even when the
command isn't invoked:

- Codebase questions → `Explore` (parallel when independent)
- Any new or changed code → `builder`
- Unclear failure → `debugger` before any fix
- Every finished change → `code-reviewer`; plus `security-reviewer` when it renders external
  content, touches URLs / storage / tokens / auth, or adds a dependency
- Every finished screen or flow → `ux-reviewer` and `responsive-reviewer`, alongside
  `code-reviewer` (delete this line if the project has no user interface)
- Task is in "Explicit cuts" in `.ai/PROJECT.md` → stop and tell the user

Exceptions where the main session may edit directly: config files, docs, and `.claude/` itself.

[As the project grows, add specialists for your stack: e.g. a `page-scaffolder` for new routes,
a `ui-builder` that knows your component library, a `ux-copy` agent held to your terms
glossary, a `migration-writer` for your database. Ask Claude to draft one from `builder.md`
and add a row here and in `/lead`'s routing table.]

**Subagents** (`.claude/agents/`)

- `builder`: implements one well-defined unit of work, following the conventions above
- `code-reviewer`: convention and correctness review after any change; reports, doesn't fix
- `security-reviewer`: exploitable findings only; verdict SAFE TO MERGE or BLOCK
- `debugger`: root-causes failures before anyone fixes them
- `ux-reviewer`: usability, hierarchy, missing states, accessibility basics; reports only
- `responsive-reviewer`: breakpoints, touch targets, overflow; hands back a manual
  render-check list it cannot run itself

**Guardrails** (`.claude/settings.json` + `.claude/hooks/git-guard.sh`)

Read-only git commands are pre-allowed; add your build tool to the allow list (e.g.
`Bash(npm *)`). A `PreToolUse` hook blocks force-pushes, pushes to `main`, `reset --hard`,
`rebase`, `branch -D`, `clean -f`, and discarding all working changes. Do those manually if
genuinely needed.
